<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stefan Nemanja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1113–1199</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Saint Simeon · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stefan Nemanja was Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from about 1166 to 1196 and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a>. He united the Serbian lands, conquering Duklja and Zachlumia, and won independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in practice. He abdicated to become the monk Simeon and with his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a> monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>; he is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja.jpg" alt="Stefan Nemanja" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stefan_Nemanja.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stefan Nemanja was born about 1113 or 1114 in Ribnica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica">Podgorica</a>, the youngest son of Zavida, a prince of Zachlumia who had been sent to Ribnica after a conflict with his brothers. Because western Zeta was under Roman Catholic jurisdiction, Nemanja was baptised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rite, although much of his later life was spent balancing Western and Eastern Christianity. In 1165 the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Comnenus</a> removed the Grand Župan Desa and gave the land to the administration of Zavida's sons, who belonged to the pro-Byzantine party. The eldest, Tihomir, became Grand Župan, and Nemanja received the areas around Ibar, Toplica and Dubočica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tihomir imprisoned Nemanja in a cave near the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a>. Nemanja escaped, took Kotor and overthrew his brother, and Manuel sent a force against him in 1168. Nemanja then turned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, but in 1172 Manuel advanced and Nemanja surrendered at Niš, barefoot, with a rope round his neck and his sword in his hands. He was confirmed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-zupan">Grand Župan</a>, led through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the emperor's triumph, and sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia">auxiliary troops</a> to Byzantine campaigns, among them the defeat at Myriokephalon in 1176. Around 1176 he also convened an assembly against the Bogumil heresy, whose leader had his tongue cut out; the sect's members suffered confiscation, expulsion and the burning of their books.</p><p>The fall of Andronikos I in 1182 freed him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>. In 1183 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary">Béla III of Hungary</a> in an offensive against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Skadar</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, and in 1189 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> at Niš, his new capital, though the emperor would promise nothing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a> defeated him at the South Morava in 1191, and a peace of 1193 arranged for his middle son Stefan to marry the emperor's niece Eudokia and to succeed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1196, at an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a>, Nemanja abdicated in favour of Stefan, leaving his eldest son Vukan in charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> and other lands. He became a monk named Simeon and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, where his youngest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> was already a monk. With permission from the Byzantine emperor they rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a>, and he issued the Charter of Hilandar. He died there on 13 February 1199 and was buried at Hilandar.</p><p>In 1207 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> brought the remains to Studenica, Nemanja's own foundation, and made peace between his brothers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">Myrrh</a> was reported to flow from the grave, and the Serbian Orthodox Church canonised him as Saint Simeon the Myrrh-streaming. His cult helped to consolidate Serbian national identity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stefan Nemanja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Grand Prince <b>Stefan Nemanja</b> of Serbia received Emperor Frederick Barbarossa and his 100,000 crusaders at Niš, his capital.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-27">27 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Stefan Nemanja</b> of the Grand Principality of Serbia annexed it around 1186, and the region, later called Zeta, became a Nemanjić appanage.</blockquote><a href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Stefan Nemanja</b> (1166–1196) defeated his brothers, annexed Duklja, Travunia and Zachlumia around 1186, and founded Studenica monastery.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-serbia">Grand Principality of Serbia</a></li><li><blockquote>Stefan, son of <b>Stefan Nemanja</b> and ruler of the Grand Principality of Serbia, was crowned king in 1217 with a crown sent by Pope Honorius III, earning the name Stefan the First-Crowned.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia-medieval">Kingdom of Serbia (medieval)</a></li><li><blockquote>Grand Župan <b>Stefan Nemanja</b> of the Grand Principality of Serbia annexed Travunia, with Duklja, around 1186.</blockquote><a href="/state/travunia">Travunia</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Miroslav, brother of <b>Stefan Nemanja</b>, ruled it in the late 12th century and commissioned the Miroslav Gospel, a masterpiece of Cyrillic illumination.</blockquote><a href="/state/zachlumia">Zachlumia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Depedogen, in what is now <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.44,19.26&amp;year=1168" aria-label="Depedogen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Stefan Nemanja"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4326 3913h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4326 3913h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.44,19.26&amp;year=1168">What was here in 1168? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
